WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.720
Hello and welcome back to the Python Core and Applied Concepts.

00:09.720 --> 00:16.880
In this video we will talk about the very basics of Act of Programming.

00:16.880 --> 00:22.840
So we will try to understand the Act of Programming by asking some very basic questions around

00:22.840 --> 00:24.760
this.

00:24.760 --> 00:31.960
For example, let's try to understand and try to understand and answer this first question

00:31.960 --> 00:36.040
which is why do we write programs?

00:36.040 --> 00:40.640
So I mean if you think carefully why do we want to write programs?

00:40.640 --> 00:44.840
So we want to write programs to get some work done.

00:44.840 --> 00:50.240
So when we say we want to get some work done, we mean we want to get some computational

00:50.240 --> 00:53.720
work done by some machines.

00:53.720 --> 00:56.840
So that's why we want to write programs.

00:56.840 --> 01:04.280
Normally those tasks for which you want to write programs should be doable manually.

01:04.280 --> 01:08.560
You should be able to, let's say if there is no computer or no machine available to

01:08.560 --> 01:14.680
you, you should be able to write mechanical procedure or you should be able to do that

01:14.680 --> 01:19.000
computation manually using pen and paper also.

01:19.000 --> 01:27.280
So since these manual process may be very tiresome, they may be very cumbersome, exhausting

01:27.280 --> 01:31.520
and monotonous, not so interesting act.

01:31.520 --> 01:37.800
That's why we normally want to relegate this kind of activity of computing some functions

01:37.800 --> 01:47.640
or some output that you want to compute based on some, let's say data, you want to relegate

01:47.640 --> 01:49.960
these kind of things to computers.

01:49.960 --> 01:55.520
So I mean we want to write program because we want to relegate these somewhat mechanical

01:55.520 --> 02:01.280
and boring task to machines which can, you know, perform maybe these tasks in a much

02:01.280 --> 02:07.960
faster way than a human can do and also, you know, in a more precise way, it can calculate

02:07.960 --> 02:09.640
those things that we wanted to produce.

02:09.640 --> 02:11.440
So that's why we write program.

02:11.440 --> 02:18.440
So one thing to note here is that we can only, you know, write programs and get things

02:18.440 --> 02:23.760
done for those kind of tasks for which we know the recipe to do those tasks, okay.

02:23.760 --> 02:29.480
So if you do not know the procedure to calculate something or compute something, you cannot

02:29.480 --> 02:31.840
instruct computers to do those things.

02:31.840 --> 02:40.840
So this is one of the very important criteria that we can only, you know, write program

02:40.840 --> 02:44.720
to do those kind of tasks which we can, you know, manually do.

02:44.720 --> 02:49.240
It requires manual and manual understanding of the task and also a mechanical procedure

02:49.240 --> 02:55.560
to accomplish this task, then only we can relegate these to computers.

02:55.560 --> 03:00.200
Now coming to the next question, you know, what are we programming?

03:00.200 --> 03:05.840
So when we say we are writing a program or we are doing the act of programming, at the

03:05.840 --> 03:11.360
other end is a real computer and real computers nothing but real machines which we are trying

03:11.360 --> 03:13.000
to program, okay.

03:13.000 --> 03:18.320
So in the process of programming, we are trying to instruct these real machines to do some

03:18.320 --> 03:22.200
computation that we desire to, you know, perform, okay.

03:22.200 --> 03:25.000
So you know, so what are we programming finally?

03:25.000 --> 03:30.680
So we are actually programming these real machines and therefore it's very important to understand

03:30.680 --> 03:32.800
the interaction with this hardware properly.

03:32.800 --> 03:40.320
If you want to convey your intention to compute something to these computers, then, you

03:40.320 --> 03:45.200
should understand this process of, you know, communication in a much more precise way,

03:45.200 --> 03:48.400
okay.

03:48.400 --> 03:53.200
So this brings us to our, you know, next question, what is a program, okay.

03:53.200 --> 03:59.720
So a program is then a mean or a method to communicate your desire of computation to

03:59.720 --> 04:02.680
these real machines that we call computer, okay.

04:02.680 --> 04:09.360
So we can communicate our, you know, intention to compute something by writing a program,

04:09.360 --> 04:15.280
you know, to these computers.

04:15.280 --> 04:22.560
So programs are sequence of instructions, they communicate computational intent to machines

04:22.560 --> 04:25.720
and therefore understanding, you know, this communication is essential, you know,

04:25.720 --> 04:30.720
are we communicating properly what we desire to compute to these machines is essential

04:30.720 --> 04:39.920
to, you know, the act of programming.

04:39.920 --> 04:42.360
So this brings us to our, you know, final question.

04:42.360 --> 04:44.280
So what is a programming language?

04:44.280 --> 04:50.280
So as we, you know, discuss just now that we write programs to communicate our intention.

04:50.280 --> 04:54.920
But then, so what is the language in which we can write these programs, okay.

04:54.920 --> 04:59.520
So like, you know, if I want to communicate with you, I use some natural language like,

04:59.520 --> 05:02.440
you know, English, Hindi or any other language.

05:02.440 --> 05:07.120
But then if I want to communicate my intention to computer, I would like to write a program.

05:07.120 --> 05:11.120
But what will be the language which I use for writing this program?

05:11.120 --> 05:13.720
So these languages are called programming languages, okay.

05:13.720 --> 05:20.560
So programming languages are like natural languages, which gives us a, you know, way to communicate

05:20.560 --> 05:25.000
our desire, which we call, you know, program to these real machines.

05:25.000 --> 05:29.520
But there is a distinction between, you know, natural languages and programming languages.

05:29.520 --> 05:32.240
So in programming languages, we cannot be ambiguous.

05:32.240 --> 05:33.920
We have to be very precise.

05:33.920 --> 05:38.920
In natural languages, when you are talking or communicating to, you know, your friend,

05:38.920 --> 05:42.320
you have already, you know, built-in context in which you communicate.

05:42.320 --> 05:46.960
So therefore, I mean, you can afford to be a little unambiguous and still your friend

05:46.960 --> 05:51.240
at the other end will be able to, you know, disambiguate what you have said and make

05:51.240 --> 05:52.240
sense of it.

05:52.240 --> 05:56.600
But computers don't have a common context of, you know, communication with you.

05:56.600 --> 06:01.440
So whatever you want to convey, you have to be very explicit and very precise in that.

06:01.440 --> 06:07.520
And programming languages, you know, gives us a mean to be precise and explicit in our

06:07.520 --> 06:08.520
communication.

06:08.520 --> 06:12.360
So these are like natural languages, but these are, you know, more formal in the sense

06:12.360 --> 06:14.640
that they are very precise.

06:14.640 --> 06:19.480
There are, you know, very strict rules of what you can write and you cannot write, you

06:19.480 --> 06:21.240
know, in a programming language.

06:21.240 --> 06:27.840
And there are very, you know, strict definition of what will be the meaning of what you write

06:27.840 --> 06:28.840
as a valid program.

06:28.840 --> 06:29.840
Okay?

06:29.840 --> 06:34.840
So there are very precise mathematical rules to define both the structure of a valid program

06:34.840 --> 06:36.840
and also the meaning of a valid program.

06:36.840 --> 06:37.840
Okay?

06:37.840 --> 06:41.280
So these two, you know, aspects of programming languages are called normally syntax and

06:41.280 --> 06:44.440
semantics of, you know, programming languages.

06:44.440 --> 06:46.800
So, you know, in a summary, what is a programming language?

06:46.800 --> 06:51.880
It's a formal language for writing program so that we can, you know, convey our computationally

06:51.880 --> 06:59.440
intention to machines and this, you know, enables communication between us and computers.

06:59.440 --> 07:03.840
And these programming languages should be defined in such a way so that there is no ambiguity

07:03.840 --> 07:07.080
that we normally have in our natural languages.

07:07.080 --> 07:12.520
Now there, you know, the programming languages can, can be categorized in a, you know, variety

07:12.520 --> 07:17.920
of, you know, different level, you know, the very low level programming languages are

07:17.920 --> 07:23.240
those programming languages, which are very close to how machine works.

07:23.240 --> 07:24.240
Okay?

07:24.240 --> 07:29.920
So, you can communicate your intention to machines by knowing how machine actually perform these

07:29.920 --> 07:30.920
now operations.

07:30.920 --> 07:36.240
So, if you are trying to communicate your intention in that way, then you will be using

07:36.240 --> 07:40.920
a low level programming languages, which are like an assembly level languages, which very

07:40.920 --> 07:44.880
closely resemble what happens inside the real computer.

07:44.880 --> 07:51.160
But then for most of us, it's not that easy to understand the whole mechanism of computation

07:51.160 --> 07:56.880
inside a computer and being able to, you know, convey our intention in form of those

07:56.880 --> 07:57.880
computation.

07:57.880 --> 08:02.880
So, we prefer, you know, most of the programmers, you know, prefer to write programs in a high

08:02.880 --> 08:08.680
level programming languages where things are more, you know, close to, you know, natural

08:08.760 --> 08:09.760
languages.

08:09.760 --> 08:14.040
And it's very easy to make sense of, you know, what, you know, we are trying to communicate

08:14.040 --> 08:18.240
to computer and then really get the, you know, task of translating, you know, these high

08:18.240 --> 08:23.080
level instruction to the low level, you know, exact instruction, which machine can execute

08:23.080 --> 08:25.280
to interpreter or compilers.

08:25.280 --> 08:30.880
So, you know, this is the distance between low level and high level.

08:30.880 --> 08:37.320
So just to summarize in, you know, low level programming, you know, languages, we work with

08:37.320 --> 08:43.120
exact machines, which you can see on the, you know, left hand side and, you know, for

08:43.120 --> 08:49.320
that we need to understand how these exact machine work, how, you know, memory addresses

08:49.320 --> 08:55.120
are handled and how, you know, data flows between memory and, you know, computing units inside

08:55.120 --> 08:56.120
the computer.

08:56.120 --> 09:01.360
So, if you know exactly which instructions, I mean, in assembly language can help us trigger

09:01.360 --> 09:06.400
these events, then we can actually write programs in low level programming languages.

09:06.400 --> 09:10.640
But on the other hand, I mean, most of us normally, you know, prefer something, you know,

09:10.640 --> 09:16.000
like, you know, right hand side where we work with a high level programming languages.

09:16.000 --> 09:21.640
And these high level programming languages help you abstract out the exact details of

09:21.640 --> 09:26.880
this machine and you can, you know, in mind, always assume for any given programming language

09:26.880 --> 09:33.440
that there is an abstract machine, which is far more simpler than the real machine in

09:33.440 --> 09:34.960
terms of, you know, thinking.

09:34.960 --> 09:41.680
So, these abstract machine helps you, you know, handle most of the complexities of this

09:41.680 --> 09:46.880
real machine by abstracting out these registers, memory and, you know, these computations

09:46.880 --> 09:51.880
in form of variable and high level data structure and how those variables are, you know, manipulated

09:51.880 --> 09:53.800
by different instructions in program.

09:53.800 --> 10:00.160
So, you know, in a sense, you can reason about the final output or the result of execution

10:00.160 --> 10:05.600
of program correctly, but without getting bothered about what's happening at a very low

10:05.600 --> 10:06.600
level.

10:06.600 --> 10:09.520
So, if you write a program in a high level programming language, this programming languages

10:09.520 --> 10:15.720
give you enough feature to abstract out unnecessary, let's say, details, which is not necessary

10:15.720 --> 10:19.600
to, you know, think about if you're, you know, concerned about the logic of your program

10:19.600 --> 10:26.920
or, you know, your program being computing the logical functions on those input data,

10:26.920 --> 10:30.040
then you can actually focus on these high level programming languages.

10:30.040 --> 10:34.960
Python is one such programming languages and hence, I mean, it gives us a nice abstraction

10:34.960 --> 10:39.480
where we can get rid of most of these low level details and we can just focus on the

10:39.480 --> 10:45.160
program logic and write correct program to achieve some computational.

10:45.160 --> 10:51.800
So, just to summarize, low level languages interact directly with hardware, which we call

10:51.800 --> 10:56.840
exact machines, low level code provides you fine-grained control because, you know, exactly

10:56.840 --> 11:01.240
how, you know, computer works, so you can actually instruct them in their language, immediate

11:01.240 --> 11:02.240
language.

11:02.240 --> 11:07.000
So, most of the time, you can be very efficient in translating your intention to what

11:07.000 --> 11:12.360
machine need to do to let's say, achieve those computational desire.

11:12.360 --> 11:16.640
On the other hand, high level languages offer greater abstraction, right?

11:16.640 --> 11:21.920
We have a simpler, you know, machine which we call abstract machine and high level code

11:21.920 --> 11:27.200
normally emphasizes on human readability and Python is one such, you know, high level

11:27.200 --> 11:29.360
programming language.

11:29.360 --> 11:35.160
So, this is, again, the same thing in, you know, one picture if we want to say things.

11:35.160 --> 11:40.640
So, we are going from these exact machines, which are real machines, which will finally

11:40.640 --> 11:46.520
perform the computation, but in order to convey our, you know, computational desire to these

11:46.560 --> 11:51.160
exact machines, we don't need to always, you know, write instruction in the language,

11:51.160 --> 11:53.120
which is very close to these machines.

11:53.120 --> 11:58.760
We can write programs in a high level, you know, programming languages and all of this

11:58.760 --> 12:02.560
can be translated easily to these languages by the interpreter and compiler.

12:02.560 --> 12:08.440
So, this gives us, you know, extreme freedom and, you know, this improves our capability

12:08.440 --> 12:14.040
to reason about the input output behavior of program at a very high level without getting

12:14.040 --> 12:21.040
bothered about what's happening at the low level at a computer.

12:21.040 --> 12:30.360
So, this is a quick recap of what we discussed today.

12:30.360 --> 12:37.200
So, we program real computers, which are physical machines and therefore programs are sequence

12:37.200 --> 12:42.360
of instructions for, you know, these machines and these instructions in a way communicate

12:42.400 --> 12:46.640
our computational desire to these computers, computers.

12:46.640 --> 12:51.720
And normally the languages which you use for, you know, conveying this computational

12:51.720 --> 12:56.280
desire are called programming languages and they can be roughly categorized into low

12:56.280 --> 12:59.960
level programming language and high level programming languages.

12:59.960 --> 13:05.080
Low level languages are very close to machine, but they are very difficult to write programming.

13:05.080 --> 13:08.840
That's why we move to high level programming languages, which gives us, you know, enough

13:08.840 --> 13:14.280
abstraction to make our life easy and write, you know, meaningful complex programs which

13:14.280 --> 13:19.120
can be executed on these real machines.

13:19.120 --> 13:27.320
So, please take your time and reflect on these ideas, so we will meet in the next video

13:27.320 --> 13:28.320
till then, goodbye.

